course: dictionary
lesson: 725
title: A name, a train, or a title?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are not ordinary words. They are names, and names start with a capital letter. Bart is almost always a name. The words around it tell you which kind of Bart is meant. In «Bart is my best friend, and he lives next door», Bart is a boy's name.
explain: Look for clues. In «Bart waited on the platform for the next train», the words platform and train show that Bart is a person waiting, not a train. When a word could mean several things, try each meaning in the sentence and see which one still makes sense.
explain: Names can also come from stories and TV. «"Don't have a cow" is American slang for "don't get so upset"; it became famous through Bart Simpson in the 1990s.» Here Bart is a cartoon character, and the sentence tells you what the saying means. A dictionary tells you the rest.
fact: Bart is a name for a boy or a man, short for Bartholomew or Bartlett.
fact: Bart Simpson is a cartoon boy, and the best-known Bart.
fact: «BART» in the USA is short for Bay Area Rapid Transit, the train system around San Francisco.
fact: «Bart.» in the UK is short for «baronet», a title written after a man's name, as in «John Smith, Bart.»
fact: Names always start with a capital letter, so Bart is written with a big B.
exercise: «Bart is my best friend, and he lives next door.» What is Bart here? | A bus | *A boy's name | A train | A cow
exercise: In San Francisco, people ride the BART to work every day. What does BART mean? | *Bay Area Rapid Transit, the train system around San Francisco | A cartoon boy | A name short for Bartholomew | A baronet's title
exercise: A letter is addressed to «John Smith, Bart.» What does Bart. show? | A cartoon character | A bus stop | A boy's name | *A title, short for baronet
test: «"Don't have a cow" is American slang.» What does the saying «don't have a cow» mean? | Don't be late for school | *Don't get so upset | Don't go to the farm | Don't feed the animals
test: Why is Bart written with a capital B? | Because it is a title | Because it is a train | Because it starts a sentence every time | *Because it is a name
exam: Read: «Bart waited for the BART at the station.» Which is true? | Bart is a bus, and BART is a title | Bart is a baronet, and BART is a cartoon | Bart is a train, and BART is a boy | *Bart is a boy's name, and BART is a train system around San Francisco
